decidable refinement constraining both. T ORCH DYNAMO guards become the runtime shadow of
these refinements (Theorem 5), and T ENSOR G UARD’s A BSTAIN verdict marks exactly the subgraphs
on which Dynamo would have broken the graph. The cost is principled: we report A BSTAIN as a
first-class verdict rather than silently claim verification.

70

Contributions. (C1) A refinement-typed calculus Tensor{s, g | φ} unifying symbolic shape
and static gradient-flag analysis under one Z3 procedure (Section 2), with classical Preservation/Progress (Theorems 10 and 11). (C2) An assume/guarantee discipline at the nn.Module
class boundary, with a contravariant/covariant subclassing rule (Section 2.2); the soundness of the
composition rule is mechanised in Lean on a 3-operator DSL (matmul, view, add); the algorithmic
discipline is implemented over the full 79-handler catalogue but is audited end-to-end only on the
3-operator fragment. (C3) An autograd-aware backward verifier catching three canonical silentzero-grad bug classes statically (Section 3.2); 500/500 static↔runtime agreement, 8/8 canonical
bugs caught, 0/50 false positives. The lattice is first-order ({has grad, no grad, ⊤}) and is
silently incorrect on parameter-sharing-under-renamed-attribute (prevalence ≤ 12% of training
scripts; Section 6); we flag this as a substantive caveat, not a footnote. (C4) A preliminary Dynamoguard inclusion lemma (Theorem 5, necessary direction only): every TG refinement variable is, on
the matching aten call, a metadata read T ORCH DYNAMO’s specialiser issues on a FakeTensor.
The mapping is shipped as data (28 rows; mechanically rechecked against the imported torch
— 28/28 pass under torch 2.9.1). The lemma is empirically audited on a 14-module corpus;
the end-to-end instantiation covers nine CNN blocks fully (without forward-signature surrogate),
while four transformer blocks use the documented surrogate because their full instantiation exceeds
constraint solving at this scale. We accordingly scope C4 as a preliminary result for the current
transformer architecture coverage. We do not claim a two-directional correspondence. (C5) A
488-block + 60-bug + 10-real-public-bug benchmark with calibrated reporting under a five-way
verdict taxonomy per tool (Section 4.1); a per-feature stress benchmark on 25 targeted cases
(Table 5) reporting that, on the real corpora, only three knobs — device-consistency, gradientflow, and low-confidence gating — move verdicts; a 25-block hybrid-mode falsification corpus
(Table 4); and an AST-skeleton diversity analysis (Kast =406 on the 488 blocks). The contribution
is restricted to those three discriminative knobs; the unused CEGAR loop and the always-satisfiable
phase encoder ship with the analyser but are not claimed as contributions. The localisation tracer is
reported as engineering rather than as a research result. (C6) A Lean 4 operator-rule audit of the
shape-transfer rule table: 28 shape-transfer rules with 11/11 previously-axiomatic soundness
lemmas closed sorry-free, the full operator-rule tree building sorry-free under lake build, and
28,000/28,000 in-envelope samples agreeing with torch 2.9.1 (Section 4.4; Table 7). The
rule table is audited; the analyser implementation, AST extractor, and backward verifier are not
audited.

