Refinement-Typed Calculus and Module Composition

We formalise T ENSOR G UARD’s abstract domain as a refinement-type system over a torch.fx-style
IR. The type of a tensor is


Tensor τ, φshape (d1 , . . . , dk ), φgrad ,
where τ is a dtype, φshape is a quantifier-free predicate over symbolic dim variables in Tshape = LIA ∪
Div ∪ BMul (linear integer arithmetic, divisibility, and bounded nonlinear multiplication restricted
to syntactically-known factors), and the grad refinement φgrad ∈ {has grad, no grad, ⊤} abstracts
requires grad ∧ is on tape. The grad lattice is flat: has grad and no grad are incomparable,
joined to ⊤. A typing context Γ binds variables to refinement types, accumulates path predicates, and
tracks a grad-context flag G ∈ {on, off} recording whether the cursor is inside torch.no grad().
The entailment Γ |= φ is discharged by Z3 on the shape projection and by the lattice on the grad
component.
Representative typing rules. We give five rules; the full set (matmul, conv1d/2d/3d, broadcast,
view, reshape, split, chunk, unbind, cat, stack, permute, transpose, einsum, indexing, slicing, scatter,
gather, repeat, expand, SDPA, LayerNorm, RMSNorm, detach, no grad context, optimizer step) is
in Section B, with pen-and-paper soundness in Section C.
