Theorem 2 (Soundness of the proof-grade verdicts). For any closed e in the supported fragment
and any heap σ |= Γ: (i) if Verify(e) = V ERIFIED, no reduction sequence from ⟨e, σ, on⟩ raises a
shape-mismatch exception, and the grad-flag claim holds at every loss.backward() site; (ii) if
Verify(e) = R EFUTED -P ROOF, every reduction sequence raises a shape- or grad-flag exception at the
witnessed subterm; (iii) if Verify(e) = C ONTRACT-V IOLATION, (ii) holds under the assumption that
every caller supplies inputs satisfying assume e ; (iv) if Verify(e) ∈ {L IBRARY-WARN, A BSTAIN},
no claim is made.
The proof (induction on reductions; full case analysis in Section C) reduces (i),(ii) to per-operator
preservation lemmas already covered by the Lean rule audit (Section 4.4), and (iii) to the same
argument inside the rely envelope assume e .

134

Subject reduction and progress. The classical Preservation and Progress theorems for FTG are
established in Section D (Theorems 10 and 11). Together they yield Theorem 12: well-typed programs
in FTG whose operator preconditions hold in σ raise no shape-mismatch exception on any reduction
sequence. Theorem 2 is the abstract-interpretation specialisation of this classical pair to the verdict
lattice; the verdict V ERIFIED corresponds to Z3 discharging all preconditions symbolically.

