The subclassing rule is a syntactic obligation: a subclass that overrides forward in a way that violates
the parent’s guarantee is rejected statically rather than silently re-analysed.
Theorem 3 (Compositional soundness; mechanised fragment). Scope. The theorem as stated is
mechanised on the same 3-operator DSL (matmul/view/add) as; it does not cover the full 79-handler
operator surface of Table 7, the AST extractor, or the Python analyser implementation. If every leaf
3
module in the call tree of an FTG
program is contract-verified in the mechanised fragment and every
composition obligation is discharged by Z3, then the whole-program verdict given by the inductive
use of S EQ, PAR, and S UB is sound in the sense of Theorem 2 restricted to the same fragment.
Theorem 4 (Monotonicity of refinement). Strengthening any single contract C(M0 ) to
C ′ (M0 ) ⊑ C(M0 ) never turns a V ERIFIED root into R EFUTED -P ROOF; the new verdict is in
{V ERIFIED, C ONTRACT-V IOLATION, L IBRARY-WARN, A BSTAIN}.

