LW block actually flips to RP, so the 12/78 ceiling is falsifiable from the paper alone. Splitting
these 12 residuals by extension class — i.e. whether the missing rule is part of the published
roadmap of the operator-rule table or instead a fragment-level extension that would broaden the
analyser’s typed-fragment surface — gives 5 roadmap-extensions (the four super().forward()
+nn.Embedding cases and the Tensor. getitem (slice) case), 6 fragment-extensions
(unbind, F.layer norm-under-permute, Sequential iteration, Dict[str,Tensor] positional
destructuring, transposed-Parameter matmul, and torch.cumsum), and 1 mixed (the const-attrgated branch unification, which needs both a roadmap rule and a fragment-level reachability
extension); a per-row classification accompanies the table in the reproducibility appendix. Concretely
the headline 0-RP triple is dominated by principled abstention rather than unrealised reasoning;
reproducibility artefact provided.
Per-block survival of the 57 Verifieds. Of the 57 V ERIFIED verdicts under the synthesised
config envelope, the user-visible (free-symbolic-config) recomputation collapses 23 to A BSTAIN
and preserves 34 as Verified; the survivors are the larger and more structurally complex set (median
forward length 51 LoC vs. 17 LoC). The per-block correspondence is in the reproducibility appendix.
On the bug corpus TG issues R EFUTED -P ROOF on 53/60 (88.3%, Wilson 95% CI [77.8%, 94.2%]);
the 10-bug real public-repo bug corpus (Table 2) is the upstream-faithful re-extract that preserves
each nn.Module class with its real init (binding constructor scalars used by the buggy view
target). On these, TG returns 7/10 R EFUTED -P ROOF at confidence ≥ 0.99 (Clopper-Pearson 95%
CI [34.8%, 93.3%], rb 003–rb 007, rb 009, rb 010) plus 1/10 at confidence 0.80 (rb 008); the
residual 2/10 (rb 001, rb 002) are silent verifieds whose buggy and correct view targets agree on
total element count for the supplied input shape, so no shape-arithmetic check could refute them.
A separate shape-pattern coverage suite of hand-distilled one-liners on which TG returns 10/10
R EFUTED -P ROOF at 0.99 is relegated to Section J as a catalogue-coverage sanity check rather than a
live-class evaluation, and is excluded from headline counts. The constructor-bound integer-attribute
envelope synthesiser that lifts the upstream-faithful corpus to 7/10 unconditional RP — a three-stage
pipeline (init-time local-scalar fold; single-dim shape-alias recogniser; shape-tuple propagator with
starred view(*new shape) expansion) — is described in Section 3.
Fair head-to-head with Pytea (modern subset). Mechanical N/A on ≥ 435/488 blocks for
the PyTorch baselines and Pytea is a structural fact about constructor-argument requirements — it
is not a TG win over those baselines. The fair head-to-head is the modern subset of the 60-bug
corpus restricted to operators in Pytea’s 2022 catalogue (N =34): TG 32/34 (94.1%, Wilson 95% CI
[80.9%, 98.4%]) vs. Pytea 22/34 (64.7%, [47.9%, 78.5%], silent-skip-corrected; see Pytea-protocol
paragraph below). A 10-point gap on N =34 is the comparable number, not the 488-block coverage
triple. The matched-pair test on this subset (Pytea-refutes is a strict subset of TG-refutes, so b=10
TG-only catches and c=0 Pytea-only) gives McNemar’s exact two-sided p=0.00195 and a pairedbootstrap 95% CI on the refute-rate difference of [+14.7 pp, +44.1 pp] around a point estimate of
+29.4 pp ; the lower bound is well above zero so the gap is not a small-N artifact. The 32/34 figure
is reproduced at verification time (not by post-hoc filtering of a verdict log) by
which AST-screens each repro for non-catalogue ops (forced-abstain on hit) and additionally forensicsscans TG’s emitted Bug.message stream for non-catalogue operator names (forced-abstain post);
the resulting fragment-fair count is recorded in the reproducibility appendix.
