Pytea baseline: catalogue confound and modern subset. 23/29 of Pytea’s misses on the 60-bug
corpus are pure operator-catalogue gaps (no Pytea handler for SDPA, RMSNorm, einsum, Conv1d/3d,
GroupNorm, BatchNorm1d, etc., verified by grep in ’s ); the remaining 6 are symbolic-fragment
weaknesses (e.g. no divisibility for view(-1)). On the modern subset of the 60-bug corpus restricted
to operators in Pytea’s 2022 catalogue (N =34), TG 32/34 (94.1%) vs. Pytea 22/34 (64.7%, silentskip-corrected) — still a significant gap, isolated from the catalogue confound.
Pytea modern-subset filter — explicit protocol. The modern-subset filter is a static AST predicate
over the 60-bug repros: a bug b is included iff every operator called from forward appears in ’s as
of commit cb02a8a (2022-04-26). The full inclusion list and the membership rule are released as
data; the membership table is committed at . The same fragment fairness is enforced on TG: TG is
restricted to its 2022-catalogue handler intersection at verification time (no TG-only operator handler
is allowed to fire), so the 32/34 figure is on the same operator surface as Pytea’s 22/34. Of Pytea’s
nominal 25 V ERIFIED, three are labelled in as uninformative (Pytea hit attribute not found and
silently skipped the offending op); we exclude these from the headline rather than counting them as
Pytea-Verified. The calibrated headline number is therefore TG 32/34 vs. Pytea 22/34 once silent
skips are removed. For completeness on the question of whether Pytea has acquired new handlers
between 2022 and the present submission, the upstream Pytea repository (ropas/pytea) has zero
commits after cb02a8a (2022-04-26): the 2022 catalogue is the 2024−26 catalogue. The 32/34 vs.
22/34 headline is therefore already the modern-mirror intersection; we record this as a structural
rather than empirical answer (verifiable from the upstream commit log).
Post-freeze generalisation. To rule out the concern that the 60-bug corpus and the 10-bug upstreamfaithful re-extracts could have been used to retro-fit handlers, we mined a post-freeze generalisation
set of fix-PRs filed strictly after the catalogue freeze date 2026-04-07 (commit 040f6f3; freeze hash
cc75834b... over, recorded in the reproducibility appendix). N =6 post-freeze upstream-faithful
repros were ported verbatim from , , and
into . TG was run with no rule edits after freeze (verified by the freeze hash). The post-freeze
headline is 3/6 Refuted-Proof at confidence ≥ 0.99 (rb pf 001, rb pf 003, rb pf 004) plus
0/6 at 0.80 and 3/6 silent verifieds (rb pf 002, rb pf 005, rb pf 006). The three remaining silent misses fall in the same constructor-bound integer-attribute envelope class flagged in
Section 6 (an int(dim*ff mult) or q len = sliding window+1 arithmetic chain whose unsoundness is on a numerical comparison the TG fragment does not currently fold), with no new
silent-miss class introduced post-freeze. The rb pf 001 result — R EFUTED -P ROOF@0.99 on
the intermediate = int(dim*ff mult) Linear-chain in/out feature-dim pattern — comes from
numeric int/float/round casts now folded when the inner expression is statically known.
