Unfiltered pre-registered post-freeze sample. The earlier post-freeze corpus (N =6) was filtered
for ”self-contained CPU repro” and so still favoured the TG fragment. We extend it to a strictly
broader N =15 unfiltered sample drawn by a pre-registered GitHub-search query (, pre-registered
2026-04-08, one day after the freeze) across,, and, taking the first 15 matching merged PRs in
chronological order with no further filter for fragment fit; out-of-fragment cases (distributed-shape,
dtype, autograd parameter sharing, data-dependent control flow) are explicitly retained as explicit A B STAIN/silent-verified entries. Table 3 reports the per-tool verdict triple. TG catches the upstream-fixed
bug on 5/15 entries (33.3%, Wilson 95% CI [15.2%, 58.3%]): rb pf 001, rb pf 003, rb pf 004,
rb uf 008, and rb uf 012. The headline catch rate is the comparable to FakeTensorMode’s 2/15
(13.3%, [3.7%, 37.9%]) and Pytea’s 3/15 (20%, [7.1%, 45.2%], silent-skip-corrected). The pairwise
Fisher-exact two-sided p-values are 0.39 (TG vs. FakeTensorMode) and 0.68 (TG vs. Pytea); the
gap is consistent with TG strictly above both execution-based baselines but not statistically separable
at α=0.05 on N =15. We report intervals explicitly here rather than treating 5/15 as a point estimate.
The headline is therefore 5/15 point above the two execution-based baselines, not a separation; we
do not rely on a Bayesian supplement to upgrade the claim. We separately report a Refuted-Proof fire
rate of 6/15 (40%): TG also fires on rb uf 010, but along an axis (device mismatch on the same
module) different from the dtype root cause of the upstream PR; we record this off-axis fire as a false
positive against ground truth and do not count it as a catch in the headline. rb pf 001 and rb uf 012
are catches enabled by the int(...)/float(...) cast folding that closes the intermediate =
int(dim*ff mult) pattern. The remaining 3/5 catches (rb pf 003, rb pf 004, rb uf 008) are
caught by the v4 path (operator-rule application without the round-6 init-time local-scalar fold or
the single-dim shape-alias recogniser): on retrofit TG-v4 binaries (catalogue freeze without the
three round-6 additions), the headline collapses to 3/15 rather than 5/15. The 2/5 round-6-only
attribution is therefore the marginal contribution of the constructor-bound integer-attribute envelope to the unfiltered post-freeze headline. FakeTensorMode catches 2/15 (13.3%, rb uf 008,
rb uf 009); Pytea catches 3/15 (20%, silent-skip-corrected). The post-freeze unfiltered catch rate
(33.3%, 95% Wilson [15.2%, 58.3%]) is the calibrated denominator; TG’s point catch rate is strictly
above the two execution-based baselines on the same unfiltered surface, although on N =15 the
head-to-head Fisher-exact comparisons are not statistically separable at α=0.05 (two-sided p=0.39
vs. FakeTensorMode, p=0.68 vs. Pytea); we report this as a calibrated-confidence result rather than
a significance claim. A power calculation on the observed point estimates — conditioning on 5/15 for
TG, 2/15 for FakeTensorMode, and 3/15 for Pytea, and adding a fresh second wave Nnew sampled
at the same per-tool rates — locates the smallest second wave at which the head-to-head Fisher exact
reaches p < 0.05 on either pair: Nnew =26 for TG vs. FakeTensorMode (one-sided), Nnew =77 for
TG vs. Pytea (one-sided), and Nnew =56 for either pair to reach a Bayes factor BF10 ≥ 10 under a
uniform prior. These are pre-conditions for a second wave, not a claim of significance on the present
N =15. Soundness footprint of the 5 catches. On a per-catch classification of the operator handlers
each catch traverses against the soundness scope of Table 7, all 5/5 catches touch at least one Leanaudited shape-transfer rule (linear for rb pf 001 and rb pf 004; view/reshape for rb uf 008;
view/permute/conv2d for rb uf 012; einsum/expand for rb pf 003). The bug-firing handler in
each case is within the in-soundness 28+3=31-handler footprint of Theorem 2; the tested-only handlers that co-occur in the same forward body (softmax for rb pf 004, unsqueeze for rb pf 003)
are not on the bug path. The full per-PR table, the cached verdict JSON, the manifest, and the verifier
are released as data and + .
Bug-corpus exclusion denominator. The historical 60-bug corpus selected from GitHub issues filtered the 1,087 initial keyword-search hits described in the reproducibility appendix
via four exclusion rules: (i) not-a-shape-bug (∼ 612, 56%: hardware/CUDA/build/installer issues mis-tagged with shape-related keywords); (ii) closed user-error (no upstream fix; ∼
178, 16%); (iii) distributed-shape bugs whose shape arithmetic crosses a process boundary
(torch.distributed.{all reduce, all gather, broadcast}; ∼ 124, 11%, all marked A B STAIN in our taxonomy and excluded by protocol); (iv) config-attribute bugs that reduce to constructor
sentinel-resolution rather than to shape arithmetic (∼ 113, 10%, the same silent-miss class flagged
above in Section 6). 60 remain. In particular, the (iii)+(iv) exclusion of ∼ 237/1,087 hits (∼ 22%)
is a known scope cap, not a sample-selection manipulation: the same exclusion is applied to the
post-freeze corpus above (e.g. rb pf 005 required no exclusion under (iii) or (iv)).
RP rate on the config-attribute exclusion slice. The 113 bugs excluded under rule (iv) are minimal
repros of 14 canonical config-attribute archetypes (head-count divisors, gated-MLP dimensions, GQA
