Per-feature stress benchmark. On the 488-block + 60-bug aggregate corpus the per-feature
ablation is a flat line: every analyser knob (CEGAR, device-consistency, phase-check, gradient-flow,
low-confidence-violations) leaves both the 53/60 bug-refute count and the 46.1% block-abstain rate
unchanged. To test whether each feature can discriminate, we built a 25-case stress benchmark (5
cases×5 features), where each case is hand-designed so that exactly one feature fires the verdict
change. Table 5 is the resulting staircase. Two features — CEGAR contract discovery and phasecheck — did not discriminate any case in the current implementation; we report this. CEGAR’s
internal predicates are stored as metadata but never surfaced as Bug objects (architectural gap between
ShapeCEGARLoop and the verdict pipeline); the phase-check encodes Or(TRAIN, EVAL), which is
always satisfiable. The other three features — device-consistency, gradient-flow, and low-confidence
violations — each move between 4 and 8 verdicts on the discriminative cases.

