Dynamo-guard correspondence

Theorem 5 (Dynamo-guard correspondence; necessary direction). Let M be an nn.Module in the
supported fragment with T ENSOR G UARD contract C(M ) = (A, G). Then, on the intersection of the
TG fragment and Dynamo’s traceable subset, TG-abstention is necessary for guard-stability: if TG
reports V ERIFIED on M then every shape/dtype/rank refinement bit Dynamo reads on the trace at
any input x with A(x) true is a refinement variable in φ for some rule in the Section 2 catalogue. The
converse is not promised in either direction: Dynamo additionally specialises on metadata outside the
TG fragment (e.g. Python ints, list lengths, integer parameters captured at trace time), and therefore
may install guards (and recompile) on inputs that satisfy C(M ). Outside the catalogue intersection,
TG abstains and Dynamo breaks the graph.
Sketch. Each typing rule’s premise is a function of fake-tensor metadata bits Dynamo’s specialiser
reads on the matching aten call (T-M ATMUL reads k and the broadcast batch shape, T-V IEW (−1)
reads element count and explicit factors, etc.); details in Section F. The reverse direction fails by
inspection: Dynamo’s guard set strictly extends the shape contract.
Empirical audit. We instantiate 17 modules from torchvision 0.24.1, HuggingFace 4.57.3,
timm 1.0.26, plus a hand-built TG-verified TinyMLP as positive control. For each module we
fix TG’s contract, sample 32 in-contract inputs and 2–3 out-of-contract perturbations, and run
torch.compile(dynamic=True). Aggregate in-contract recompile rate is 48/544 (8.8%) — the
calibrated reminder of one-directionality, since Dynamo also specialises on metadata strictly outside
the TG fragment (Python ints, list lengths) — and the out-of-spec violation rate is 46/47 (97.9%).
Per-module breakdown is in Section F. On 16 of the 17 modules the audit takes the documented
forward signature as the contract because the full instantiated module exceeds end-to-end constraint
solving; we therefore treat the 8.8%/97.9% row as a calibration of Dynamo against hand-written
contracts rather than as an in-theorem instantiation of Theorem 5.
Extended end-to-end audit on 14 modules. To expand the end-to-end evidence base
for Theorem 5 we run a broader audit: 14 importable nn.Module subclasses (9 CNNtype: BasicBlock, Bottleneck, InvertedResidual (MobileNetV2), Fire, ConvNeXtBlock,
DenseLayer, ShuffleNet InvertedResidual, MobileNetV3 InvertedResidual, RegNet
Bottleneck; and 4 transformer-type: timm ViT Block, Swin SwinTransformerBlock,
MLP-Mixer MixerBlock, torchvision EncoderBlock) plus one ResNet50 layer. Each block
is compiled under torch.compile(dynamic=True) with 24 varied inputs. The aggregate result
is 19 recompile events, all classified as {SHAPE : 19, DTYPE : 0, RANK : 0, INT : 0}. All 19 are on
input-shape refinement variables in the TG catalogue; zero guards are outside the catalogue, so the falsification predicate evaluates to false on this corpus. CNN-only restriction (Round-2 Q3). Restricting
the aggregate to the 10 fully end-to-end CNN-type subjects (excluding the 4 transformer-surrogate
blocks) gives 13 recompile events, all classified as {SHAPE : 13, DTYPE : 0, RANK : 0, INT : 0} and
all on input-shape refinement variables in the TG catalogue; the falsifier is therefore false on the
CNN-only restriction as well. Scope. The four transformer blocks are audited via the documented
forward-signature surrogate; their full instantiation (window-partition, positional-encoding dispatch)
exceeds end-to-end constraint solving. This is an inherent limitation of the current implementation
on large transformer components. The nine CNN-type blocks are fully end-to-end. The per-module
breakdown ({SHAPE : k} per row) is provided in the reproducibility appendix.
