Falsification predicate for Theorem 5. A measurement that would have falsified the necessary direction is: ∃ in-contract recompile r with r.guard kind ∈ {SHAPE, DTYPE, RANK} and
r.guard var ∈
/ catalogue(M ). The extended 14-module audit finds zero such events; the falsifier
evaluates to false on this corpus. An adversarial custom op that reads a shape bit not declared by any
rule could in principle fire the falsifier on future modules.
Audit on a strictly larger module population. We extend the necessary-direction empirical
surface to a strictly larger candidate population: importable nn.Module blocks drawn from
torchvision 0.24.1, HuggingFace 4.57.3, and timm 1.0.26, run in a subprocess-isolated worker
under torch.compile(dynamic=True) with a 240s wall-clock kill, 3 warm-up inputs, and 7 incontract follow-ups per module. The same falsification predicate as above is evaluated on each
successfully-instantiated module: a SHAPE/DTYPE/RANK guard on a variable outside the catalogue catalogue(M ) would falsify the necessary direction. The aggregate falsifier rate is reported
as #(out-of-catalogue SHAPE/DTYPE/RANK guards) / #(SHAPE/DTYPE/RANK recompiles),
and a per-module breakdown (status, recompile count, guard-kind histogram, and the boolean
falsifier flag) is provided in the reproducibility appendix. Of 107 candidate modules, 55 completed
Dynamo warm-up; 35 exceeded the 240s wall-clock kill and 17 failed warm-up. Across the 55
successful modules we observed 72 in-contract recompiles, all classified as integer / SymInt specialisations (kind I NT); 0 SHAPE/DTYPE/RANK guards were observed and 0 modules falsified
the necessary direction (falsifier rate 0/72 = 0). Graph-break WON ’ T CONVERT log lines, which
dynamo emits when a function cannot be traced at all, are excluded from guard classification because
they correspond to conversion failures rather than recompilation guards.

532

Mutation-testing rate. To complement the four hand-picked TCB faults of Theorem 2 with an
automated robustness measurement, we sweep 50 subprocess-isolated single-edit AST mutations of
the analyser core (comparison flips, boolean-operator flips, arithmetic-operator swaps, small-integer
constant +1, boolean constant flip) and rerun the 60-bug regression for each mutant; a mutant is
counted iff at least one bug verdict differs from the clean baseline (or the mutated source fails to load).
The aggregate rate is 3/50 = 6.0%: three Boolean- constant flips raise a RuntimeError on the first
seven bugs, and the remaining 47 mutants survive — they sit on arithmetic / comparison handler
paths the 60-bug corpus does not exercise. This bounds the analyser-level robustness on the headline
corpus by an automated single-edit measurement and is deliberately weaker than the four-fault TCB
pair (which audits hand-picked faults on the construct each fault is most likely to corrupt); the two
together form an automated ∪ hand-picked robustness pair rather than a single anecdotal footprint.

