envelope; the in-fragment agreement is therefore an implementation-agreement check, and the
precondition-discovery question is answered by the boundary check above (zero silent-through
samples on ∼2,400 off-envelope mutators across 10 rules; see ). The soundness theorem applies
tightly to the 28 Lean-audited plus 3 pen-and-paper handlers; on the 488-block real-source corpus, 11
of the 57 Verified verdicts and 25 of the 128 C ONTRACT-V IOLATION verdicts touch only handlers in
this set (36/185 in-soundness verdicts in total), while 33 + 72=105/185 touch at least one of the 48
tested-only handlers (per-block scope table in the reproducibility appendix). The remaining Verified
verdicts touch only operators outside any of the three soundness scopes, which we flag as a known
TCB obligation rather than as an in-theorem result.
TCB fault-injection footprint. Theorem 2 carries the calculus, not the implementation: the AST
extractor, backward verifier, and Z3 dispatch sit in the trusted computing base. We bound the largest
verdict-flip a single deliberate fault in any of these components could cause on the headline corpora
by an exposure scan: for each audited fault we count how many corpus items exercise the construct
the fault mis-handles. Exposure is a strict upper bound on the number of RP verdicts the fault
could flip to silent V ERIFIED. The audited single-faults are F1 view(*new shape) star-expansion
mis-binding (AST), F2 Tensor.add mis-classified as out-of-place (backward verifier), F3 cat/stack
dim negation flip (Z3 dispatch), and F4 a Conv2d output-formula off-by-one (analyser handler).
On the 60-bug corpus the exposures are 0/60 (F1), 0/60 (F2), 2/60 (F3), and 7/60 (F4) – the
star-expansion and in-place-add patterns are absent from every self-contained repro, so under either
fault the 53/60 headline is unchanged; under F4 it could degrade by at most 7 to 46/60, and only
when the conv-channel rule is on the bug path. We additionally instrument each of the four faults at
the source level – F1 mis-rebinds the star-expanded shape spec inside the analyser’s view-handler, F2
marks the in-place trailing-underscore class as functional, F3 negates the dim-normalisation sign in
the cat/stack handler, and F4 substitutes (L+2P-K)/S for (L+2P-K)/S+1 in the conv-output handler
– and rerun the full 60-bug regression. The measured RP→V ERIFIED flip counts are 0/60 (F1), 0/60
(F2), 0/60 (F3), and 0/60 (F4), strictly tighter than the exposure ceilings: F3 and F4 do trigger their
respective handlers on 2 and 7 bugs, but the faulty shape value is then re-caught by another handler on
the same path, so no RP verdict actually flips. Measured flip is the operationally meaningful quantity;
exposure is the worst-case envelope. On the 488-block corpus the exposures are 5, 18, 18, and 19
(denominator 137 resolved-source blocks); since the headline on this surface is 0 unconditional RP,
the fault-flip ceiling here is on the LW/CV side rather than on RP. The full exposure scan and the
per-fault measured-flip rerun are in the reproducibility appendix.
Backward verifier on real importable models. The backward verifier’s 500/500 agreement on randomly-generated small modules is supplemented by a 10-module realworld sweep in :
the predicted requires grad topology of every parameter of
resnet18,, vit b 16, bert-base-uncased, gpt2, distilbert-base-uncased, t5-small,
whisper-tiny, clip-vit-base-patch32, and wav2vec2-base matches the runtime topology
recovered by an actual loss.backward() call on 10/10 models with 0 false positives. None of
these models exercise torch.utils.checkpoint or explicit parameter sharing; those regimes are
flagged as future work. The first-order grad-flag lattice {has grad, no grad, ⊤} rules out reasoning
about parameter-sharing-induced double counting and gradient-checkpointing recomputation, and we
have renamed the contribution accordingly throughout the paper.
Grad-flag silent-error audit on Track-E modules. A static source inspection of the 16 importable
Track-E modules (the 17-module Theorem 5 audit, minus 2 with import errors) finds 0/16 with
torch.utils.checkpoint usage and 0/16 with renamed-attribute parameter sharing (the pattern where two self.X attributes point to the same underlying nn.Parameter). The grad-flag
silent-misclassification regime (Section 6) is therefore not present in this specific set of modules. The population-level prevalence is reported separately as an AST-grep sweep over 2,908
importable Python source files in the TRANSFORMERS 4.57.3 + TIMM 1.0.26 source trees (sweep
date 2026-04-29; query: union of five renamed-attribute patterns self.X = self.Y.weight,
self.X = self.Y.bias, self.X.weight = self.Y.weight (in-place rebind), self.X =
nn.Parameter(self.Y.weight...), self.X.data = self.Y.weight.data). The result is
0/2,908 (0.00%) renamed-attribute hits; 5/2,908 (0.17%) import torch.utils.checkpoint;
333/2,908 (11.45%) use tied weights keys or tie weights/ tie or clone weights. The
