Related Work

T ENSOR G UARD sits at the intersection of refinement types, abstract interpretation for ML, and the
PyTorch shape ecosystem.
Refinement and dependent types. Liquid Haskell [Vazou et al., 2014a], F⋆ [Swamy et al., 2016],
Dafny [Leino, 2010], Lean [de Moura and Ullrich, 2021], Dependent Haskell [Eisenberg, 2016], and
singletons [Eisenberg and Weirich, 2012] establish the SMT-decidable / dependent-typing line;
Hasktorch applied type-level naturals to deep learning. We take Liquid Haskell’s SMT-decidable design, specialise the refinement language to PyTorch tensor metadata, and add the grad-flag dimension.
Abstract interpretation and shape verifiers for ML. Ariadne [Dolby et al., 2018] pioneered abstract shape inference for TensorFlow; DeepPoly [Singh et al., 2019] and AI2 [Gehr et al., 2018] certify
robustness on values assuming shapes are correct; jaxtyping/torchtyping are runtime checkers.
TG differs by symbolic, ahead-of-tracer shape reasoning that additionally models requires grad.
PyTorch- and autograd-specific tooling. FakeTensorMode [FakeTensor, 2024] and
torch.export [torch.export, 2024] symbolically execute graphs with metadata-only tensors but require the program to run under a tracer and do not reason about requires grad
flow across module boundaries. TorchDynamo [Ansel et al., 2024] installs runtime guards;
T ENSOR G UARD’s contracts are the static refinement-type analog (one-directional, Theorem 5).
Pytea [Lee et al., 2020] is the closest comparator in the no-execution / Z3 design space; we evaluate
it on both corpora (Table 1). Pytea targets shape errors only, lacks requires grad reasoning, has no
Lean operator-rule audit, and is not designed around A BSTAIN as a first-class verdict; on the bug
corpus it refutes 27/60 versus TG’s 53/60 and on the block corpus it is N/A on 435/488 for the
same constructor-inapplicability reason as torch.fx.
Assume/guarantee and contracts. Jones [Jones, 1983], Meyer [Meyer, 1992], and Findler–
Felleisen [Findler and Felleisen, 2002] established rely/guarantee, Design-by-Contract, and contract
boundaries in functional languages. We import these with shape/grad refinements as the contract
language at the nn.Module class boundary. A nine-axis comparison is in Table 6.
