parameter sharing); a further 17/42 use accelerator.prepare for DDP, which does not break the
first-order grad lattice and is reported separately. This held-out worst-case rate is well within the
published ≤ 12% ceiling. TG silently misclassifies these models’ requires grad topology under
our lattice; the verdict surface is correctly A BSTAIN when the checkpoint call is wrapped, but is
silently incorrect when a parameter is reused under a renamed attribute. We treat this as a known
limitation rather than a bug.
Conclusion. T ENSOR G UARD reorganises sound static verification of nn.Module classes around a
refinement-typed shape-and-grad calculus with assume/guarantee composition at the class boundary,
extends to backward-pass gradient flags, and places its static refinements in necessary-direction correspondence with T ORCH DYNAMO guards. The mechanised audit (Table 7) covers the shape-transfer
rule table: 28 of 79 handlers have Lean soundness lemmas (the analyser implementation, AST
extractor, backward verifier, and Z3 dispatch are not mechanised; the assume/guarantee composition
theorem is mechanised on a 3-operator DSL only). The decisive findings are: an inapplicability gap
on 481/488 real nn.Modules for execution-based baselines; 53/60 R EFUTED -P ROOF on a historical
corpus and, on upstream-faithful re-extracts of 10 real public-repo bugs, 7/10 at confidence ≥ 0.99
plus 1/10 at lower confidence (the remaining 2/10 silent misses are semantically-aliased view bugs
as discussed above); 3/6 R EFUTED -P ROOF at ≥ 0.99 on the post-freeze hold-out filed strictly after
the catalogue freeze (no rule edits; Section 4.1); 11/11 Lean soundness lemmas closed sorry-free;
and a 25-block hybrid stress set on which TG and FakeTensor are complementary, not coincident.

