Dynamo-guard correspondence: full proof

The (necessary-direction) proof is by induction on the typing rules of Section 2. For each operator
the input metadata read by Dynamo’s fake-tensor execution is exactly the metadata referenced by
the rule’s premise: T-M ATMUL reads the k-dim and the broadcast batch shapes, which are the same
quantities Dynamo specialises on at the aten::matmul call; T-V IEW (−1) reads the input’s element
count and the explicit reshape factors, which are the quantities Dynamo’s ShapeEnv guards on. The
converse fails by inspection of Dynamo’s guard emitter: guards are also installed on metadata strictly
outside the TG fragment (Python ints, list lengths, integer parameters captured at trace time), so an
in-contract input may still trip a non-shape guard. Outside the catalogue intersection TG abstains
and Dynamo reports a graph break; the empirical audit (Section 4) shows on 17 real modules the
in-contract recompile rate is 8.8% (48/544) — a calibrated reminder of the one-directionality — and
the OOS violation rate is 97.9% (46/47).
